window.docContents[183].resolve({"/Constructions/#section-exercises-constructions":{"contents":"1. Show that a Cayley graph defined in a commutative additive group is simply a\ncirculant graph.\n\nexample (G : Type*) [AddCommGroup G] (s : Set G) :\n    addCayley s = circulantGraph s := by\n  sorry\n\n\n2. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices is a circulant graph with the following\ndifferences. Hint: first show that the set of squares in ZMod 17 is\n{0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16}. After ext the goal mentions a new variable of\ntype ZMod 17, but decide only works on statements with no free variables. Instead\nuse decide +revert, which first reverts the variable and then checks the resulting\nstatement ∀ y : ZMod 17, … case by case.\n\nlemma paleyGraph₁₇_eq_circulant' :\n    paleyGraph (ZMod 17) = circulantGraph ({1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16}) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n3. Prove that in Q[n + 1] the vertex (v, b₁) is adjacent to (v, b₂) if and only\nif b₁ ≠ b₂.\n\nexample {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) (b₁ b₂ : Bool) :\n    Q[n + 1].Adj (v, b₁) (v, b₂) ↔ b₁ ≠ b₂ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n4. Prove that the union (i.e. ⊔) of the complete bipartite graph between V and\nW with the disjoint union ⊕g (i.e. sum) of complete graphs on V and W is\nthe complete graph on V ⊕ W.\n\nexample {V W : Type*} : completeBipartiteGraph V W ⊔ (⊤ ⊕g ⊤) = ⊤ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n5. Prove that the complete 1-partite graph (on ℕ) is empty.\n\nexample : multiNat 1 = ⊥ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n6. Prove that the blow-up of a complete graph is a complete multipartite graph.\n\nexample {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) :\n    (⊤ : SimpleGraph ι).blowUpGraph V = completeMultipartiteGraph V := by\n  sorry\n\n\n7. Prove that the cycle graph C[3] is the complete graph on Fin 3. (Mathlib has\nthis as cycleGraph_three_eq_top, but try to prove it directly.)\n\nexample : C[3] = (⊤ : SimpleGraph (Fin 3)) := by\n  sorry\n\n\n8. Prove that the disjoint union of empty graphs is empty.\n\nexample {ι : Type*} {V : ι → Type*} : Σg (fun i => (⊥ : SimpleGraph (V i))) = ⊥ := by\n  sorry\n\n\n9. Prove that every left copy of a vertex is isolated in the Mycielskian of the\nempty graph. (So mμ[⊥] is a star on the right copies together with the isolated\nleft copies.)\n\nexample {V : Type*} (u : V) (x : Option (V ⊕ V)) :\n    ¬ mμ[(⊥ : SimpleGraph V)].Adj (u ₗ) x := by\n  sorry\n\n\n10. Prove that the cycle graph C[n + 2] is the circulantGraph {1}. (Mathlib has\nthis, for C[n + 1], as cycleGraph_eq_circulantGraph, but prove it directly.)\n\nexample (n : ℕ) : C[n + 2] = circulantGraph {1} := by\n  sorry\n\n\n1. Show that a Cayley graph defined in a commutative additive group is simply a\ncirculant graph.example (G : Type*) [AddCommGroup G] (s : Set G) :\n    addCayley s = circulantGraph s := by\n  ext u v\n  rw [addCayley_adj', circulantGraph_adj]\n  aesop\n2. Show that the Paley graph on 17 vertices is a circulant graph with the following\ndifferences.lemma paleyGraph₁₇_eq_circulant :\n    paleyGraph (ZMod 17) = circulantGraph ({1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16}) := by\n  have : {y : ZMod 17 | IsSquare y} = {0, 1, 2, 4, 8, 9, 13, 15, 16} := by\n    ext\n    constructor <;> decide +revert\n  simp [this, circulantGraph_eq_erase_zero]\n3. Prove that in Q[n + 1] the vertex (v, b₁) is adjacent to (v, b₂) if and only\nif b₁ ≠ b₂.example {n : ℕ} (v : HypercubeVert n) (b₁ b₂ : Bool) :\n    Q[n + 1].Adj (v, b₁) (v, b₂) ↔ b₁ ≠ b₂ := by simp\n4. Prove that the union (i.e. ⊔) of the complete bipartite graph between V and\nW with the disjoint union ⊕g (i.e. sum) of complete graphs on V and W is\nthe complete graph on V ⊕ W.example {V W : Type*} : completeBipartiteGraph V W ⊔ (⊤ ⊕g ⊤) = ⊤ := by\n  ext u v\n  cases u <;> cases v <;> simp\n5. Prove that the complete 1-partite graph (on ℕ) is empty.example : multiNat 1 = ⊥ := by\n  ext ⟨i, _⟩ ⟨j, _⟩\n  simp [Subsingleton.elim i j]\n6. Prove that the blow-up of a complete graph is a complete multipartite graph.example {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) :\n    (⊤ : SimpleGraph ι).blowUpGraph V = completeMultipartiteGraph V := rfl\n7. Prove that the cycle graph C[3] is the complete graph on Fin 3. (Mathlib has\nthis as cycleGraph_three_eq_top, but try to prove it directly.)example : C[3] = K[3] := by\n  ext\n  decide +revert\n8. Prove that the disjoint union of empty graphs is empty.example {ι : Type*} {V : ι → Type*} : Σg (fun i => (⊥ : SimpleGraph (V i))) = ⊥ := by\n  ext ⟨i, u⟩ ⟨j, v⟩\n  simp\n9. Prove that every left copy of a vertex is isolated in the Mycielskian of the\nempty graph. (So mμ[⊥] is a star on the right copies together with the isolated\nleft copies.)example {V : Type*} (u : V) (x : Option (V ⊕ V)) :\n    ¬ mμ[(⊥ : SimpleGraph V)].Adj (u ₗ) x := by\n  rcases x with _ | (v | v) <;> simp\n10. Prove that the cycle graph C[n + 2] is the circulantGraph {1}. (Mathlib has\nthis, for C[n + 1], as cycleGraph_eq_circulantGraph, but prove it directly.)example (n : ℕ) : C[n + 2] = circulantGraph {1} := by\n  ext u v\n  grind [cycleGraph_adj, circulantGraph_adj]\n\n\nend SimpleGraph\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Constructions","header":"3.8. Exercises","id":"/Constructions/#section-exercises-constructions"},"/Distance/#section-distance-invariants-under-isomorphism":{"contents":"Since isomorphisms preserve distances they preserve eccentricities, and hence the\ndiameter and the radius are isomorphism invariants.\n\nlemma Iso.eccent_eq {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (e : G ≃g H) (u : V) :\n    H.eccent (e u) = G.eccent u :=\n  (e.toEquiv.iSup_comp (g := H.edist (e u))).symm.trans (iSup_congr (e.edist_eq u))\n\n\nlemma Iso.ediam_eq {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (e : G ≃g H) :\n    G.ediam = H.ediam :=\n  (iSup_congr fun u => (e.eccent_eq u).symm).trans\n    (e.toEquiv.iSup_comp (g := H.eccent))\n\n\nlemma Iso.radius_eq {W : Type*} {H : SimpleGraph W} (e : G ≃g H) :\n    G.radius = H.radius :=\n  (iInf_congr fun u => (e.eccent_eq u).symm).trans\n    (e.toEquiv.iInf_comp (g := H.eccent))\n\n\nIn a vertex-transitive graph (Vertex Transitivity) all\neccentricities coincide, so the radius equals the diameter and every vertex is\ncentral. We use this below to compute the radius of the hypercube from its diameter.\n\nlemma IsVertexTransitive.eccent_eq (h : G.IsVertexTransitive) (u v : V) :\n    G.eccent u = G.eccent v := by\n  obtain ⟨f, hf⟩ := h u v\n  rw [← hf, f.eccent_eq]\n\n\nlemma IsVertexTransitive.radius_eq_ediam [Nonempty V] (h : G.IsVertexTransitive) :\n    G.radius = G.ediam :=\n  radius_eq_ediam_iff.mpr\n    ⟨G.eccent (Classical.arbitrary V),\n      fun u => h.eccent_eq u _⟩\n\n\nlemma IsVertexTransitive.center_eq_univ [Nonempty V] (h : G.IsVertexTransitive) :\n    G.center = Set.univ :=\n  center_eq_univ_iff_radius_eq_ediam.mpr h.radius_eq_ediam\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Distance\u0009Eccentricity, Diameter and Radius","header":"11.2.1. Distance Invariants under Isomorphism","id":"/Distance/#section-distance-invariants-under-isomorphism"},"/Introduction/#section-installation":{"contents":"If you haven't done so already please install Lean\nand clone a copy of the repository https://github.com/jt496/GraphTheoryInLean. Reading through\nthis guide within VSCode will allow you to understand more easily how the example\ncode works, via the InfoView messages.\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Introduction","header":"1.1. Installation","id":"/Introduction/#section-installation"},"/Maps/#section-comaps":{"contents":"Given a function f : V → W, we can push a graph G : SimpleGraph V forwards along\nf or pull a graph H : SimpleGraph W back along f. The former is called the\nmap of G along f and the latter is called the comap of H along f.\n\nOf these, SimpleGraph.comap is used frequently in constructions of concrete graphs,\nso we start with it. The SimpleGraph.map construction is less frequently used for\nconstructing concrete graphs, but appears often in proofs; we return to it in\nMaps, once we have met graph homomorphisms.\n\nGiven f : V → W and G : SimpleGraph W, G.comap f is the SimpleGraph V where\ntwo vertices are adjacent iff their images under f are adjacent in G.\n\n#source SimpleGraph.comap\nexample {V W : Type*} (f : V → W) {v₁ v₂ : V} (G : SimpleGraph W) :\n  (G.comap f).Adj v₁ v₂ ↔ G.Adj (f v₁) (f v₂) := comap_adj ..\n\n\nTwo examples of graphs defined using comap are the complete multipartite and\nblow-up graphs. (If you read the starred section\nComplete Multipartite Graphs And Blow Ups, you have already seen\nthem.) Two vertices are adjacent in a complete multipartite graph iff they are in\ndifferent parts.\n\n#source completeMultipartiteGraph    -- SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) := .comap Sigma.fst ⊤\nexample {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) {u v : Σ i, V i} :\n    (completeMultipartiteGraph V).Adj u v ↔ u.fst ≠ v.fst := Iff.rfl\n\n\nTwo vertices are adjacent in the blow-up of a graph G iff they are in parts that\nare adjacent in G.\n\n#source blowUpGraph -- abbrev blowUpGraph {ι : Type*} (G : SimpleGraph ι)\n                    --    (V : ι → Type*) : SimpleGraph (Σ i, V i) :=\n                    --                          SimpleGraph.comap Sigma.fst G\n\n\nexample {ι : Type*} (V : ι → Type*) {u v : Σ i, V i} (G : SimpleGraph ι) :\n    (G.blowUpGraph V).Adj u v ↔ G.Adj u.fst v.fst := Iff.rfl\n\n\nIn both cases the adjacency relation is defined by pulling back the adjacency\nrelation of a graph using SimpleGraph.comap and the map Sigma.fst : Σ i, V i → ι\nthat sends a vertex to its part.\n\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps","header":"5.2. Comaps","id":"/Maps/#section-comaps"},"/Maps/#section-copies":{"contents":"We already met graph homomorphisms A →g B and graph embeddings A ↪g B. Copy A B\nfalls between the two: it is the type of injective graph homomorphisms from A to\nB.\n\nWhile A ↪g B captures the type of induced copies of a graph A in a graph B,\nCopy A B captures the type of not-necessarily-induced copies of A in B.\n\n#source Copy\n\n\nNote that the copies are labelled: an element of Copy A B records where each\nvertex of A goes. So if A has nontrivial automorphisms then a single subgraph of\nB isomorphic to A is the image of several elements of Copy A B, one for each\nautomorphism of A. We return to this distinction in\nCounting Copies.\n\nAn induced copy of A in B (i.e. f : A ↪g B) gives rise to a copy of A in\nB.\n\n#source Embedding.toCopy -- .. (f : A ↪g B) : Copy A B := f.toHom.toCopy f.injective\n\n\nNote that any copy of a complete graph is necessarily induced so we can always\nupgrade a Copy ⊤ G to a graph embedding ⊤ ↪g G.\n\nexample {V W : Type*} {G : SimpleGraph V} (c : Copy (⊤ : SimpleGraph W) G) :\n    (⊤ : SimpleGraph W) ↪g G := c.topEmbedding\n\n\nWe say that A is contained in B, written A.IsContained B or A ⊑ B, iff\nthere exists a Copy A B.\n\n#source IsContained -- (A : SimpleGraph α) (B : SimpleGraph β) := Nonempty (Copy A B)\n\n\nIf B contains no copy of A then we say that B is A-free, written\nA.Free B. This follows the convention of extremal graph theory, where \"A-free\"\nmeans containing no copy of A at all (as in \"triangle-free\"). Beware that\nstructural graph theory often uses \"A-free\" to mean containing no induced copy of\nA (as in \"claw-free\"). For that notion we need a separate definition, which we give\nin Claw Free Graphs.\n\n#source Free -- abbrev Free (A : SimpleGraph α) (B : SimpleGraph β) := ¬A ⊑ B\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Maps\u0009Morphisms","header":"5.3.3. Copies","id":"/Maps/#section-copies"},"/Subgraphs/#section-matching-number-of-the-hypercube":{"contents":"The hypercube Q[n + 1] has a natural perfect matching: pair each vertex with the\none obtained by flipping its first coordinate, so v[0, …] is matched with\nv[1, …]. (Flipping the first coordinate rather than the last lets us build the\nmatching by recursion on n.)\n\nRather than reason about the hypercube directly, we isolate the underlying general\nfact about box products: if M is a perfect matching of G, then copying M into\nevery H-fibre of G □ H is a perfect matching of G □ H, for any graph H, whose\nedges play no role at all.\n\n/-- Copy a subgraph M of G into every H-fibre of G □ H: the vertex (v, b)\nis present iff v ∈ M.verts, and (a, b) — (c, b) is an edge iff a — c is an\nedge of M. This is the box product of M with the edgeless (but fully spanning)\nsubgraph on H. -/\n@[simps]\ndef Subgraph.boxProdLeft {β : Type*} (M : G.Subgraph) (H : SimpleGraph β) :\n    (G □ H).Subgraph where\n  verts := {x | x.1 ∈ M.verts}\n  Adj x y := M.Adj x.1 y.1 ∧ x.2 = y.2\n  adj_sub h := boxProd_adj.2 (Or.inl ⟨M.adj_sub h.1, h.2⟩)\n  edge_vert h := M.edge_vert h.1\n  symm := ⟨fun _ _ ⟨h1, h2⟩ => ⟨h1.symm, h2.symm⟩⟩\n\n\nIf M is a perfect matching of G then M.boxProdLeft H is a perfect matching of\nG □ H: the unique neighbour of (v, b) is (w, b), where w is the M-partner\nof v.\n\nlemma Subgraph.IsPerfectMatching.boxProdLeft {β : Type*} {M : G.Subgraph}\n    (h : M.IsPerfectMatching) (H : SimpleGraph β) :\n    (M.boxProdLeft H).IsPerfectMatching := by\n  rw [Subgraph.isPerfectMatching_iff]\n  rintro ⟨v, b⟩\n  obtain ⟨w, hw, huniq⟩ := h.1 (h.2 v)\n  refine ⟨(w, b), ⟨hw, rfl⟩, ?_⟩\n  rintro ⟨c, d⟩ ⟨hadj, rfl⟩\n  exact Prod.ext (huniq c hadj) rfl\n\n\nThe recursive perfect matching of the hypercube is now immediate.\n\n/-- A perfect matching of Q[n + 1] defined recursively: ⊤ on Q[1], and on\nQ[n + 2] the fibre-copy (Subgraph.boxProdLeft) of hypercube.matching n along\nK₂. -/\ndef hypercube.matching : (n : ℕ) → Q[n + 1].Subgraph\n  | 0 => ⊤\n  | n + 1 => (hypercube.matching n).boxProdLeft ⊤\n\n\nA graph, or rather its top subgraph, is a perfect matching iff every vertex has\ndegree one.\n\nlemma Subgraph.isPerfectMatching_top_iff [G.LocallyFinite] :\n    (⊤ : G.Subgraph).IsPerfectMatching ↔ ∀ v : V, G.degree v = 1 := by\n  simp [isPerfectMatching_iff, SimpleGraph.degree_eq_one_iff_existsUnique_adj]\n\n\nBy induction hypercube.matching n is a perfect matching of Q[n + 1].\n\nlemma hypercube.matching_isPerfectMatching :\n    ∀ n : ℕ, (hypercube.matching n).IsPerfectMatching\n  | 0 => Subgraph.isPerfectMatching_top_iff.mpr <| hypercube_isRegularOfDegree _\n  | n + 1 => (hypercube.matching_isPerfectMatching n).boxProdLeft ⊤\n\n\nSince Q[n + 1] has 2 ^ (n + 1) vertices, this perfect matching gives\nν Q[n + 1] = 2 ^ n.\n\nlemma hypercube.matchingNum_succ (n : ℕ) : ν Q[n + 1] = 2 ^ n := by\n  rw [(hypercube.matching_isPerfectMatching n).matchingNum_eq]\n  have h : Nat.card (HypercubeVert (n + 1)) / 2 = 2 ^ n := by\n    rw [Nat.card_eq_fintype_card, card_hypercubeVert, pow_succ]; grind\n  exact_mod_cast h\n\n\nend hypercubematching\n\n\n","context":"Graph Theory in Lean\u0009Subgraphs\u0009Matching Number","header":"9.5.1. Matching Number of the Hypercube","id":"/Subgraphs/#section-matching-number-of-the-hypercube"}});